WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:04.580
The initial character design for the first
"Sonic the Hedgehog" film was met with overwhelming

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:09.610
backlash in 2019, and it seemed like the video
game-turned-film was doomed.

00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:13.950
However, after being delayed by Paramount
so the team could redesign the titular blue

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:17.560
hedgehog, the film went on to have a record-setting
opening weekend.

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:22.519
Now, with two sequels and a spin-off series
on the horizon, the adaptation has successfully

00:00:22.519 --> 00:00:23.919
turned things around.

00:00:23.919 --> 00:00:27.869
The reception for the new, final trailer for
"Sonic the Hedgehog 2" is a whole lot warmer

00:00:27.869 --> 00:00:32.290
than that original, much-maligned trailer,
with many fans eager to see what's next for

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:33.290
the franchise.

00:00:33.290 --> 00:00:37.930
Jim Carrey, who seldom stars in sequels, will
even be returning as the evil mad scientist

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:40.340
Doctor Ivo "Eggman" Robotnik.

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:44.760
The upcoming adventure film will follow Sonic,
voiced by Ben Schwartz, as he faces off against

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:46.620
a vengeful Robotnik.

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:51.580
This time, though, each has a partner in crime:
Tails, voiced by Colleen O'Shaughnessey, and

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:53.390
Knuckles, voiced by Idris Elba.

00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:57.650
If Sonic and Tails don't beat Robotnik and
Knuckles to the Master Emerald, it could mean

00:00:57.650 --> 00:00:58.960
the end of the world.

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:02.790
With the stakes higher than ever, the final
trailer for "Sonic the Hedgehog 2" offers

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:06.600
another glimpse at the explosive search for
this all-powerful emerald.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:09.170
Here's what you might've missed in the action-packed
new trailer.

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:12.060
"Sonic the Hedgehog" has always had a rocking
soundtrack.

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:16.009
In the newest trailer for "Sonic 2," one of
the first scenes shows James Marsden's Thomas

00:01:16.009 --> 00:01:20.880
Wachowski attending a wedding in Hawaii, but
right in the middle of the ceremony his phone

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:21.880
rings.

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:24.259
"So help me Thomas!"

00:01:24.259 --> 00:01:25.619
"Sorry, sorry."

00:01:25.619 --> 00:01:30.499
Astute fans may have picked up on this auditory
cue, but for the uninitiated, the ringtone

00:01:30.499 --> 00:01:34.619
is actually the music from the first level
of the original "Sonic" video game.

00:01:34.619 --> 00:01:38.369
This little tune comes to us from the Green
Hill Zone, which was also referenced in the

00:01:38.369 --> 00:01:42.130
first film as the town Sonic hides in: Green
Hills, Montana.

00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:46.359
One of the major driving forces of both the
"Sonic" movies and video games are mysterious

00:01:46.359 --> 00:01:48.530
stones known as Chaos Emeralds.

00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:52.659
In the new trailer, we see that Dr. Robotnik
has discovered what he refers to as a source

00:01:52.659 --> 00:01:56.579
of ultimate power, which is revealed to be
the green Chaos Emerald.

00:01:56.579 --> 00:02:00.950
These particular gems, within the game series,
are imbued with fantastic powers like time

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:06.389
manipulation or the ability to transform its
holder into a being of vast cosmic power,

00:02:06.389 --> 00:02:11.390
as illustrated by Sonic's yellow-furred iteration
that is effectively unstoppable in the video

00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:12.890
game "Sonic 3."

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:16.991
Sonic wishes to protect the Chaos Emeralds,
while Dr. Robotnik wants to use their power

00:02:16.991 --> 00:02:19.280
to remake the world in his own twisted image.

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:23.520
Knuckles is the official guardian of these
powerful relics, and his tribe of anthropomorphic

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:25.890
animals is tasked with protecting them.

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:30.450
The trailer sees Dr. Robotnik use a Chaos
Emerald to power his army of machines, while

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:35.341
also radiating the awesome power of the Emeralds
himself, which is illustrated by the green

00:02:35.341 --> 00:02:37.530
arcs of lightning across his body.

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:41.780
Hopefully this means that we might get a glimpse
of 'Super Sonic' at some point in "Sonic 2."

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:46.150
As longtime "Sonic" gamers have been pointing
out online, there's a moment in the new trailer

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:50.650
that is almost identical to a classic "Sonic
the Hedgehog 2" level called the Sky Chase

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:55.870
Zone, in which Tails and Sonic operate a red
and white biplane dubbed "The Tornado" through

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:57.460
various obstacles and enemies.

00:02:57.460 --> 00:03:02.620
In the new trailer, we see nearly a shot-for-shot
recreation of it, with Sonic even standing

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:05.760
on top of the plane's wings just like he does
in the game.

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:09.970
While "Sonic the Hedgehog" found a place in
the gaming world as Sega's answer to Nintendo's

00:03:09.970 --> 00:03:15.040
Mario, it resembled most early '90s games
by focusing more on the gameplay than the

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:16.040
storyline.

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:20.290
Collecting golden rings and avoiding obstacles
all while going really fast wasn't something

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:23.010
that essentially needed a story in those days.

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:26.680
However, in a small throwback moment from
the new trailer, audiences were treated to

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:29.620
a sweet callback to the game that changed
all of that.

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:33.660
In a moment with epic slow motion and an obligatory
shout-out to the MCU …

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:36.000
"Oh great, the Winter Soldier."

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:40.680
… Sonic jumps from a cave at the top of
a mountain and uses a makeshift snowboard

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:42.430
to flee from pursuers.

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:45.610
When Sonic jumps on his board, he lets out
a recognizable cheer:

00:03:45.610 --> 00:03:47.120
"Hoo hoo hoo!"

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:49.129
Why is this cool?

00:03:49.129 --> 00:03:53.930
The exclamation goes back to 1998, with the
release of "Sonic Adventure," which was the

00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:58.910
first to truly introduce 3D gameplay, the
first to be story-driven, and the first to

00:03:58.910 --> 00:04:02.200
include a full voice over, including Sonic's
little cheer.

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:03.830
"Woo hoo!

00:04:03.830 --> 00:04:05.460
Woo hoo!"

